  ],
  "slug": "scalable-commodity-1353",
  "summary": "Rhythm methods are now benchmarked on empirically estimated count models, alongside circadian eQTL mapping in GTEx and organ-specific aging clocks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is the explicit treatment of measurement structure in rhythm inference and evaluation. A benchmark of 12 differential rhythmicity methods from 5 packages uses synthetic RNA-seq count data with mean-dispersion trends estimated empirically, so that methods are tested under empirically grounded count models. A genotype-by-rhythm cosinor framework applied to GTEx v10 identifies 167 circadian eQTLs across 28 tissues and links them to traits via PheWAS, extending rhythm analysis to genetic control and tissue specificity. Organ-specific proteomic aging clocks for eight mouse organs, applied to RNA and spatial transcriptomes, form a related but distinct application in which organ-dependent age signals are the object of inference. What is new is that empirically estimated structure is used both to construct the evaluation and to frame genetic and tissue-level inference.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-29"
}
